<h2>Quarterly anchors</h2>

<p>Five things happen every quarter, regardless of which calendar quarter we're in:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Client QBRs.</strong> Quarterly business reviews with each monthly client. 30 minutes scheduled, usually Google Meet, agenda follows the template in <a href="/sop/retention-and-upsell/">M9</a>. Batched in the third month of the quarter to spread the load.</li>
  <li><strong>One published case study.</strong> Per <a href="/sop/case-studies-and-pr/">M7</a>. Published in the second month of the quarter so the case study has time to circulate before QBR season.</li>
  <li><strong>Partner check-ins.</strong> Brief sync with each active partner, per <a href="/sop/partner-program/">M8</a>. Usually a 15-minute call or a thoughtful email exchange.</li>
  <li><strong>Vendor / SaaS review.</strong> Run the inventory in <a href="/sop/tools/">tools &amp; access</a>. Confirm renewal dates, kill anything unused, document any tool migrations.</li>
  <li><strong>Reviews-asking sweep.</strong> Per <a href="/sop/reviews-and-testimonials/">M6</a>. Identify the 3-5 clients who've had a notable recent moment (a launch, a positive QBR, a successful campaign) and ask each for a Google review.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Monthly anchors</h2>

<p>Every month, regardless of which:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>First Monday: invoice run.</strong> All monthly-plan invoices generated and sent before noon. Per <a href="/sop/billing/">phase 11</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>First Tuesday: month-ahead planning.</strong> 30-minute block to look at the next four weeks. Confirm cadence is loaded, identify any unusual scheduling (holidays, client launches, travel). Output is a one-page "this month" plan.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-month (around the 15th): editorial planning.</strong> Confirm the next two blog post topics, draft the social repurposing rhythm for each, queue newsletter topics. Per <a href="/sop/content-marketing-ops/">M4</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-month: maintenance check on every active client site.</strong> Per <a href="/sop/maintenance/">phase 09</a>. Lighthouse scan, accessibility spot-check, broken-link scan, security advisories review.</li>
  <li><strong>Last Friday: metrics review + retro.</strong> 60 minutes. Pull dashboard numbers (new leads, discovery calls booked, proposals sent, monthly recurring revenue, content traffic). Compare to month-ago and quarter-ago. Write a short retro: what worked, what didn't, what to change.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Weekly cadence</h2>

<p>Five-day workweek. Each day owns specific kinds of work; spillover is the exception, not the norm.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Day</th><th>Anchor</th><th>Owns</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Monday morning</td><td>Cold-outreach batch (~25 prospects, 4-touch sequence). Newsletter draft on alternate weeks.</td><td><a href="/sop/cold-outreach/">M1</a> + <a href="/sop/email-newsletter/">M5</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Monday afternoon</td><td>Client work block (deep work).</td><td>Active engagements</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Client work block (deep work, full day if possible).</td><td>Active engagements</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Wednesday morning</td><td>Maintenance pass on every active client site.</td><td><a href="/sop/maintenance/">phase 09</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Wednesday afternoon</td><td>Content production (blog draft, edit, repurpose).</td><td><a href="/sop/content-marketing-ops/">M4</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Client work block (deep work, full day if possible).</td><td>Active engagements</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Friday morning</td><td>Admin: invoicing, expense logging, inbox zero, any client comms backlog.</td><td><a href="/sop/billing/">phase 11</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Friday afternoon</td><td>Newsletter ships on alternate weeks. Week-ahead planning. Shutdown checklist.</td><td><a href="/sop/email-newsletter/">M5</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sunday afternoon</td><td>Social-media batch &mdash; 4-5 posts drafted and scheduled for the week ahead.</td><td><a href="/sop/social-media/">M3</a></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Saturday is off. The Sunday-afternoon social batch is a 90-minute exception specifically because batching mid-week breaks the deep-work blocks.</p>

<h2>Daily cadence</h2>

<p>Every workday follows roughly the same shape. Variation week-to-week is in the middle blocks; the bookends stay constant.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Morning (8:30-9:00):</strong> Inbox triage. 30 minutes max. Reply to anything urgent, snooze anything that needs a thoughtful response into the afternoon block, archive everything else. End the block with a clear inbox.</li>
  <li><strong>Morning deep work (9:00-12:00):</strong> Three uninterrupted hours on whatever the highest-leverage client work or content production is for the day. Email closed, phone in another room, no ad-hoc meetings. This is the most productive block of the day and it stays protected.</li>
  <li><strong>Midday (12:00-1:00):</strong> Lunch + a walk. The walk is the source of most of the blog post angles I write &mdash; phone in pocket, no podcasts, just the time to actually think.</li>
  <li><strong>Afternoon client comms (1:00-2:00):</strong> Respond to any DMs, social comments, slower-thread emails, scheduled calls. The discovery-call slots, when booked, sit in this hour.</li>
  <li><strong>Afternoon work block (2:00-5:00):</strong> Whatever the day's secondary priority is. Content production on Wednesdays, admin on Friday afternoons, more client work on Tuesdays and Thursdays.</li>
  <li><strong>End-of-day shutdown (4:45-5:00):</strong> 15-minute structured shutdown. Open the day's task list; mark everything done that's done; pull tomorrow's top three items into a small written list; close every browser tab. Walk away with a clean state. The shutdown is what makes the next morning's deep-work block possible.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Holiday + PTO map</h2>

<p>Recurring closures, with the prep windows that precede each:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Thanksgiving week (US):</strong> closed Wednesday through Friday. Auto-responder activates Tuesday evening with the return date and the emergency-protocol link from <a href="/sop/emergencies/">phase 10</a>. Prep window: the Friday before, draft the auto-responder copy and confirm scheduled posts cover the week.</li>
  <li><strong>Late December:</strong> closed December 24 through January 2. Auto-responder activates December 23 evening. Prep window: first week of December, finalize closure dates with each active client, queue any posts that should publish during the closure, batch any year-end client communications.</li>
  <li><strong>Independence Day week (US):</strong> closed July 3 through July 5 if it falls on a weekday. Standard auto-responder.</li>
  <li><strong>Memorial Day, Labor Day:</strong> closed Monday only. Auto-responder activates Friday evening.</li>
  <li><strong>Per-year PTO:</strong> two weeks total, taken in 3-5 day chunks. Each chunk has a backup contact arrangement (named in advance) for genuine emergencies and a longer auto-responder window than the holidays use.</li>
</ul>

<p>The auto-responder copy and the PTO handoff checklist both live in <a href="/sop/email-templates/">email templates</a> and <a href="/sop/checklists/">checklists</a> respectively.</p>

<h2>What feeds what</h2>

<p>The cadences nest into each other. The flow, top of funnel to delivery:</p>

<pre>cold outreach (Monday)            local network (monthly events)
       │                                    │
       └──────► discovery calls ◄───────────┘
                     │
                     ▼
              proposal (within 24h)
                     │
                     ▼
              agreement signed + first invoice
                     │
                     ▼
              onboarding (week 1) ──► design (week 2-3)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      build (week 3-5)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      pre-launch QA + launch
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      monthly maintenance ◄── runs forever
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      quarterly QBR
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      annual review + rate notice</pre>

<p>The marketing cadences (content, newsletter, social, partners, reviews) run in parallel, all feeding into "discovery calls booked" as the proximal output. The lifecycle cadences (onboarding through offboarding) process the engagements that result.</p>

